These results suggest that the accuracy or the quality of other downstream analyses depends not only on the presence of relevant genes but also on how effectively they are ranked before selection. Methods like GF-IDF that incorporate contextual relevance appear to be better suited to highlight meaningful biological signals within literature-derived gene lists. Furthermore, GF-IDF is able to accommodate larger gene sets without a strong performance deterioration. The drop in MRR indicates that as gene lists expand, frequency alone is insufficient to filter out less relevant entries, reducing the method’s precision in comparison to a more context-based selection. The slight improvement at higher \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
